{"id": "q8", "claim": "c3", "kind": "claim", "form": "spot-wrong-version", "level": "situation", "sections": ["s4", "s8"], "question": "Suppose the Persistent SAE's state update (Equation 5) were changed to drop the weighting on the new token-wise activation. Take the persistence value every feature has at the start of training, from the initialisation and parameterisation in Appendix B.3. Under a constant activation a, what steady-state magnitude would the altered rule give, what does the paper's actual rule give, and which property claimed in Appendix B.3 would be lost?", "answer": "The logits are initialised to 0.5, so λ = 0.999·σ(0.5) ≈ 0.62. Without the (1−λ) factor, the steady state is a/(1−λ) ≈ 2.6a. The paper's EMA rule converges to a for every λ. The lost property is that persistence does not systematically inflate state magnitude: under the altered rule, magnitude would grow with λ, reaching about 1000a near the 0.999 cap.", "rubric": ["Uses the paper's initial logit 0.5 and cap 0.999 to get λ ≈ 0.62, giving a steady state of about 2.6a under the altered rule", "States that the paper's actual EMA rule converges to a regardless of λ", "Identifies the lost property: persistence no longer leaves state magnitude independent of timescale (magnitude grows with λ)"]},
{"id": "q9", "claim": "c3", "kind": "claim", "form": "connect-sections", "level": "situation", "sections": ["s4", "s8"], "question": "Take the persistent-state L0 that Figure 8 reports once Persistent SAE training stabilises, and the per-token activation budget shared by every SAE in the paper. Roughly how many times larger is the state L0 than the budget? Why does this not mean the Persistent SAE breaks the paper's matched-sparsity comparison?", "answer": "The state L0 settles at about 400–500, while the per-token BatchTopK budget is k = 20, so the state is about 20–25 times larger. Sparsity is imposed only on the token-wise activation a, not on the state h. Because h integrates past sparse updates and decays them slowly, many coordinates stay nonzero at once, while each per-token a still respects the k = 20 budget.", "rubric": ["States a ratio of roughly 20–25× (state L0 about 400–500 vs k = 20)", "Identifies that sparsity is imposed on the token-wise activation a, not on the state h", "Explains that h accumulates past sparse updates, so many coordinates are nonzero simultaneously without violating the per-token budget"]},
{"id": "q11", "claim": "c4", "kind": "claim", "form": "connect-sections", "level": "situation", "sections": ["s3", "s5"], "question": "Using Table 1's smoothness values for the High and Low feature groups on Pythia, compare how the Persistent SAE's groups and the Temporal SAE's groups are ordered. How does this ordering mirror the result of the Section 5.1 check against empirical persistence, and what does it imply about learned coefficients versus a predefined partition?", "answer": "On Pythia, the Persistent SAE's high-λ group has smoothness 0.05 versus 0.20 for its low group (lower is smoother), so slow features are about 4× smoother. The Temporal SAE's predefined high-level group scores 0.18 versus 0.11 for its low-level group, so it is actually less smooth. This mirrors Section 5.1: λ quintiles are cleanly ordered by empirical persistence, while the Temporal SAE's predefined groups are not clearly separated. Learned coefficients therefore track real timescales, whereas a fixed partition does not guarantee that its 'high-level' group is slow.", "rubric": ["Reports that the Persistent SAE's High group is much smoother than its Low group (≈0.05 vs ≈0.20), while the Temporal SAE's High group is not smoother than its Low group (≈0.18 vs ≈0.11)", "Links this to the Section 5.1 finding that higher-λ quintiles show slower decay of empirical persistence, whereas the Temporal SAE's predefined groups are not separated", "Concludes that learned coefficients capture data timescales better than a predefined partition"]},
{"id": "q22", "claim": "c7", "kind": "integration", "form": "connect-sections", "level": "situation", "sections": ["s4", "s5", "s11"], "question": "The prompt-injection study runs on Gemma-2-2B. Treat Table 5's Gemma features F-11205 and F-7307 as representative slow and fast features. Using the persistent-state update and their reported λ values, what weight does each give a new token-wise activation, and what fraction of its state does each keep per token? Use this to explain the case study's finding about which state is the strongest detector at the end of the untrusted span versus far later.", "answer": "F-11205 (λ ≈ 0.918) gives new evidence only about 8% weight and keeps about 92% of its state per token. F-7307 (λ ≈ 1.9×10⁻⁶) gives essentially 100% weight to the current token and keeps almost nothing. The slow state therefore takes in the injected span only weakly, so fast and standard representations are stronger at d=0. The fast state, however, loses the signal once the injected text is no longer current, while the slow state's high retention preserves it at longer distances.", "rubric": ["Uses Table 5's values: about 0.08 new-evidence weight and 0.92 retention for F-11205, and about 1 and about 0 for F-7307", "Explains that the slow state's weak uptake makes it weaker at d=0, while the fast state tracks the current token strongly", "Explains that the slow state's high retention preserves the signal at later distances, while the fast state forgets immediately"]},
{"id": "q23", "claim": "c3", "kind": "integration", "form": "connect-sections", "level": "situation", "sections": ["s3", "s10", "s4", "s11"], "question": "Across the Gemma examples in Table 5, roughly how many orders of magnitude separate the largest and smallest learned λ? Which Appendix D result about standard SAE features, measured across the paper's lag bands, anticipates such a spread? What would a single persistence coefficient shared by all features fail to capture?", "answer": "λ ranges from about 0.918 (F-11205) to about 1.9×10⁻⁶ (F-7307), a spread of roughly 5–6 orders of magnitude. Appendix D found that when standard SAE features are ranked by their average R_j(τ) in short (τ≤16), medium (32–64) and long (≥128) lag bands, the distributions span nearly the full unit interval in every band, far more than in the shuffled control. A single shared coefficient would impose one timescale and could not represent both near-instant and long-lived features.", "rubric": ["States a spread of about 5–6 orders of magnitude (≈0.92 down to ≈2×10⁻⁶)", "Cites Appendix D: persistence distributions in short/medium/long lag bands span nearly the full unit interval, more than in the shuffled control", "Concludes that a shared coefficient would impose one timescale and miss feature-specific timescales"]},
{"id": "q24", "claim": "c5", "kind": "integration", "form": "connect-sections", "level": "situation", "sections": ["s5", "s11", "s14"], "question": "In the prompt-injection case study, the fast-feature state's probe accuracy falls toward chance beyond the untrusted span, together with certain other representations. Which representations does the paper report behaving the same way? Using the largest λ among Table 5's low-λ Gemma examples and the first checkpoint after d=0 in Appendix H, explain why the fast state behaves like them.", "answer": "The standard SAE representations also fall toward chance. The largest low-λ Gemma value is about 1×10⁻³ (F-9996). At the first later checkpoint, 50 tokens on, the retained fraction is about (10⁻³)⁵⁰, effectively zero, so the fast state equals the current token-wise activation, just like a standard SAE's per-token encoding. Once the injected text is no longer the current content, neither contains the injection evidence the d=0 probe relied on.", "rubric": ["Identifies the standard SAE representations as also falling toward chance", "Uses λ ≈ 1e-3 and the 50-token checkpoint to conclude essentially zero retention, so the fast state is just the current token-wise activation", "Concludes that once the injected text is gone, this current-token representation lacks the injection signal, so the d=0 probe drops toward chance"]}
]}
